(*
# Dump Static
The whitelist entries no cache can produce, staged into the output tree by
copying them.

Dependency-free in the same sense as the rest of `utils/cache/` - no
`WaspLib/main.simba`, so no `String` helper methods. Use the Lape built-ins
(`LowerCase`, `SameText`, `Trim`) and `raise` a `'DumpStatic: ...'`-prefixed
string on failure.

## These are not a gap, they are a measurement

Fifteen of the twenty-four whitelist paths are currently staged from static
input rather than produced by a cache
dumper, and twelve of those were never produced by *anything* automated -
`fonts/fonts.zip`, the four `images/*` files, `images/skills.zip`,
`finders/house_layout.zip`, `jsons/consumables.{json,zip}`, `jsons/monsters`,
`jsons/monsters.zip` and `jsons/weapons.json` are curated data with no
`Simba*Dumper` behind them at all.

Three more were established as unreproducible rather than merely unimplemented
(`docs/phase3-research-sprites.md`):

| file | finding |
|---|---|
| `finders/prayer.zip` | 71% of its colours occur in **no frame** of the 11681-sprite index - screen captures, hand-cleaned |
| `finders/overheads.zip` | localises to sprite archive 440 by colour, but no nearest-neighbour downscale exceeds **0.224** similarity |
| `finders/spells.zip` | 73.4% bit-exact cache sprites, but its name -> sprite-id mapping exists in **no source file**, and **9 sprite ids are claimed by 2-5 entries**, so some committed rows are wrong |

## `jsons/gear.json` is cache-derived

`dumpgear.simba` now generates it directly from the ITEM archive. Its twelve
slots are a deterministic function of `wearPos1/2/3`, lower-cased item names,
deduplication and byte-lexicographic sorting; cache 2625 reproduces the former
static file byte for byte.

## The parity property this buys

A copied file has, by construction, the committed CRC32. So these fifteen
entries in a generated `hashes.json` should equal the ones in
`wasp-assets/hashes.json` - the only entries in the whole manifest of which that
is true, since every generated zip is written by {ref}`TZipWriter`, whose
deflate is not the JDK's. That makes the copy step self-checking end to end:
`dev/tests/420_static.simba` asserts against the committed manifest, not against
its own recomputation.

### ...for fourteen of the fifteen

**`jsons/monsters` is the exception, on every non-Windows host, and it is a
`.gitattributes` artefact rather than a defect.** `wasp-assets/.gitattributes`
normalises with `* text=auto` followed by `*.* text eol=lf`; the second rule
pins every file **with a dot in its name** to LF everywhere. `jsons/monsters` is
the only whitelisted file without one - the repo has exactly two extensionless
tracked files, it and `LICENSE` - so only `text=auto` applies and its
working-tree form follows the checkout platform.

Measured: the checked-out file here is 38869 bytes of LF, CRC32 `81455CA5`;
the committed manifest says `E4AC5B2E`, which is exactly the CRC32 of the same
content with CRLF (41119 bytes, 2250 lines). The manifest was written on
`windows-latest`.

Nothing is done about it. `hashes.json` must be the true CRC32 of the file we
actually shipped, and copying the bytes we were given is what makes that true.

## Source in the standalone generator

The standalone `asset-generator` vendors these inputs under `static/` and
passes that directory explicitly to `DumpStaticAssets`. A generator run is
therefore self-contained and never needs to read or alter `wasp-assets/`.
*)
{$DEFINE WL_CACHE_DUMP_STATIC_INCLUDED}
{$IFNDEF WL_CACHE_DUMP_COMMON_INCLUDED}{$INCLUDE_ONCE dumpcommon.simba}{$ENDIF}

const
(*
## GENERATED_ASSETS
```pascal
GENERATED_ASSETS: TStringArray
```
The whitelist paths a dumper in this directory claims. Everything else in the
whitelist is static, which is how {ref}`StaticAssetPaths` derives its list
instead of transcribing one.

| path | dumper |
|---|---|
| `jsons/item-definitions.zip` | `dumpitemdefs.simba` |
| `jsons/gear.json` | `dumpgear.simba` |
| `map/objects.zip` | `dumpobjects.simba` |
| `map/mapdata.zip` | `map/mapdata.simba` |
| `map/npcs.zip` | `dumpnpcs.simba` + `dumpnpcspawns.simba` (third-party spawn source) |
| `finders/items/items.zip`, `finders/items/data/{hash,id,item}.txt` | `dumpitems.simba` |

All five rows are live dumpers rather than static copies. This list still lives
separately from the registry so the static step can never overwrite a generated
artifact. `map/npcs.zip` remains the one network-backed generated path: its
coordinates come from `mejrs/data_osrs` and do not exist in the cache. A failed
fetch is non-fatal and leaves it absent from the manifest, which is the honest
state for that one run.
*)
  GENERATED_ASSETS: TStringArray = [
    'finders/items/data/hash.txt',
    'finders/items/data/id.txt',
    'finders/items/data/item.txt',
    'finders/items/items.zip',
    'jsons/gear.json',
    'jsons/item-definitions.zip',
    'map/mapdata.zip',
    'map/npcs.zip',
    'map/objects.zip'
  ];

(*
## StaticAssetPaths
```pascal
function StaticAssetPaths(hashable, generated: TStringArray): TStringArray;
```
`hashable` minus `generated`, preserving `hashable`'s order. With
`AssetHashablePaths(ASSET_WHITELIST)` and {ref}`GENERATED_ASSETS` that is the 15
static paths.

Derived rather than written out, so that registering a new dumper shrinks the
copy set by itself. The alternative - a second hand-maintained list - fails
silently in the worst possible direction: a path left in both sets would have
its freshly generated content overwritten by a stale copy on the next run.

`hashable` is expected to have been through `AssetHashablePaths` already, which
is what drops the `hashes.json` self-reference; this function only removes
`generated`. Both sides are trimmed and compared with `SameText`, the ASCII
`equalsIgnoreCase`, because the caller may well be handing over
`FileReadLines(whitelist.txt)`.

Example:
```pascal
WriteLn(StaticAssetPaths(['a.zip', 'b.zip'], ['b.zip']));  // ['a.zip']
```
*)
function StaticAssetPaths(hashable, generated: TStringArray): TStringArray;
var
  i, k: Integer;
  entry: String;
  skip: Boolean;
begin
  for i := 0 to High(hashable) do
  begin
    entry := Trim(hashable[i]);
    if entry = '' then Continue;

    skip := False;
    for k := 0 to High(generated) do
      if SameText(entry, Trim(generated[k])) then
      begin
        skip := True;
        Break;
      end;

    if not skip then
      Result += entry;
  end;
end;

(*
## _StaticParentOf
```pascal
function _StaticParentOf(rel: String): String;
```
Everything in `rel` before its last `/`, or `''` when there is none.

`'finders/items/data/hash.txt'` -> `'finders/items/data'`; `'hashes.json'` ->
`''`. The caller feeds the result to {ref}`TAssetDump.Ensure`, which is
recursive, so the three-level case needs no special handling.

Written against `/` and not `PATH_SEP` deliberately: whitelist entries are
always spelled with forward slashes, and this runs before the separator is
rewritten.
*)
function _StaticParentOf(rel: String): String;
var
  i: Integer;
begin
  for i := Length(rel) downto 1 do
    if rel[i] = '/' then
      Exit(Copy(rel, 1, i - 1));
  Result := '';
end;

(*
## DumpStaticAssets
```pascal
function DumpStaticAssets(srcDir: String; var dump: TAssetDump; paths: TStringArray): Integer;
```
Copies every `paths` entry from `srcDir` into `dump`'s tree, creating the
directories on the way, and returns how many were copied - which is always
`Length(paths)`, because a missing source raises rather than being skipped.

That is the opposite of {ref}`WriteHashesJSON`'s rule, and deliberately so. A
whitelist file our dumpers do not produce yet is a known, temporary hole and is
worth omitting quietly; a *static* file missing from the source directory means
the source directory is wrong, and quietly staging fourteen of fifteen would
surface hours later as one unexplained manifest entry.

Each copy is verified by CRC32 before it counts. `FileCopy` reports success from
the API call, not from the bytes on disk, and a truncated static asset is
exactly the failure this whole step is supposed to be incapable of.

Re-running over a populated tree is fine: `FileCopy` overwrites, unlike
`TImage.Save`.

Example:
```pascal
dump.Setup('/tmp/assets');
DumpStaticAssets('/path/to/wasp-assets',
                 dump,
                 StaticAssetPaths(AssetHashablePaths(ASSET_WHITELIST),
                                  GENERATED_ASSETS));
```
*)
function DumpStaticAssets(srcDir: String; var dump: TAssetDump; paths: TStringArray): Integer;
var
  base, rel, parent, src, dst: String;
  i: Integer;
begin
  if Trim(srcDir) = '' then
    raise 'DumpStatic: no static asset directory - pass --static=<dir> ' +
          'pointing at a wasp-assets checkout';

  base := PathIncludeTrailingSep(_DumpNormSep(srcDir));

  for i := 0 to High(paths) do
  begin
    rel := Trim(paths[i]);
    if rel = '' then Continue;

    src := base + _DumpNormSep(rel);
    if not FileExists(src) then
      raise 'DumpStatic: no "' + rel + '" under "' + base + '" - this file is ' +
            'not derivable from the cache and has to be supplied';

    parent := _StaticParentOf(rel);
    if parent <> '' then
      dump.Ensure(parent);

    dst := dump.Path(rel);
    if not FileCopy(src, dst) then
      raise 'DumpStatic: could not copy "' + src + '" to "' + dst + '"';

    // FileCopy answers for the call, not for the bytes.
    if HashFile(EHashAlgo.CRC32, dst) <> HashFile(EHashAlgo.CRC32, src) then
      raise 'DumpStatic: "' + rel + '" did not survive the copy intact';

    Inc(Result);
  end;
end;
